////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// Acid Test: Chain Rule Params
//
// The parameterized ChainsRule form (Slice 2 of the chains-first-class
// plan, 2026-07-28), on the AlgebraRule(NoMatrixOps) precedent:
// `ChainsRule(op, ...)` parses to the Application (ChainsRule op ...)
// and enables transitivity ONLY for the families of the listed
// operators - here ChainsRule(lt) enables the inequality ladder and
// nothing else.  Chains of every family still SPLIT into steps (the
// steps below all validate from their Assumes), and pure-= chains are
// always enabled (they are the ChainsRule machinery itself), but a
// disabled family's transitive conclusion is not emitted.
//
// Each section below uses its own disjoint constants so that rules and
// instantiations cannot cross-justify between sections.

Declare =, |, <
Rule: :{ ChainsRule(lt) }

➤ "an inequality chain: its family is enabled, so the conclusion is emitted"
{
  Declare a, b, c
  {
    Assume a = b
    Assume b < c
    { a = b < c } ✔︎
    a < c ✔︎
  }
}

➤ "a pure = chain is always enabled"
{
  Declare u, v, w
  {
    Assume u = v
    Assume v = w
    { u = v = w } ✔︎
    u = w ✔︎
  }
}

➤ "a divisibility chain: steps still split and validate, but its family is not listed, so no transitive conclusion"
{
  Declare x, y, z
  {
    Assume x | y
    Assume y | z
    { x | y | z } ✔︎
    x | z ✗
  }
}
